Anna —

It is done, and I will tell you how it ended, because the ballads are already getting it wrong.

Nobody sang. Nobody made a speech. Wren put an arrow through the last lamp by the throne so the Duke's knight was fighting Aldric in half-dark, and while every eye and blade in that hall was busy, Lady Harrow — sixty years old, boar-spear and all — walked up the dais steps and sat down on the throne of the kingdom like a woman resting her feet at market.

"Witnessed," said the cook, Tam, from the doorway.

And that was the end of the war. The garrison grounded arms almost gratefully, the way tired men put down something heavy. The Winter Duke stood in his mourning grey looking at the arithmetic — his silver spent, his mirror-soldiers surrendering, an old woman on his stolen chair — and to his single, solitary credit, he did not make us kill him. There will be a trial. The market women, I am told, are already spitting practice.

The boy-king comes down from the hills in the spring. Lady Harrow holds the chair warm until then and terrifies the treasury into honesty. Aldric has gone back to not talking. Wren is unbearable at cards again.

I re-read my first letter, the one about the toll going up. It did go up, sister. Corben. Harrowfield. Every mile of that road. The price of a crown, paid mostly by people who never wore one.

Which is why it matters who sits in the chair. Come down from the hills in spring — I'll show you a kingdom with the receipts kept honest.

Your sister, in one piece,
— Elin

